  "transcript": "# The Online Safety Act: implementation and impact\n\n## Scale and persistence of harms\n> Experts say harms against women and girls are widespread and rising, and the new law hasn’t yet reduced them.\n\nChair: Nine months after the children’s rules started, Ofcom found no big drop in children’s exposure. Has the Online Safety Act reduced harms against women and girls?\nAlexandra Whiston-Dew: No. Our law firm still sees very serious online harm to women and girls. The problem is endemic, and some platforms appear unwilling to comply.\nJanaya Walker: We support regulation, but implementation and limits of the Act are a problem. New harms like AI-generated abuse are expanding fast.\nJanaya Walker: Research shows one in three women report online violence; it’s nearly half for 16–24-year-olds. Referrals to services have risen since the Act came in.\nSophie Mortimer: The law made intimate image abuse a priority. Hashing and guidance are steps forward, but platforms haven’t meaningfully improved removal, and reports keep rising.\nSophie Mortimer: In 2025 our helpline logged over 20,000 images and that keeps growing; most victims are women and many cases go unreported.\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: There was potential for real change, but it hasn’t happened yet. Search engines and other services still facilitate extreme material—often inconsistently.\n\n\n## Guidance versus binding rules\n> All witnesses urged that Ofcom’s guidance on violence against women and girls must be made enforceable to be effective.\n\nLord Storey: Ofcom says the VAWG guidance was collaborative. Should it be made statutory?\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: Yes. The guidance is useful but not legally binding. Making it a code or part of mandatory risk assessments would strengthen it.\nSophie Mortimer: Participation in StopNCII rose from 17 to 30 partners since the guidance, but that’s under 1% of platforms in scope. Mandatory rules are needed.\nJanaya Walker: Guidance remains voluntary though Parliament expected stronger measures. The Act left too much good practice as optional.\nAlexandra Whiston-Dew: Anonymous image boards and purpose-built sites won't self-regulate. Guidance won’t touch those; legal obligations are needed.\nLord Storey: Clare suggested platforms do the minimum to appear compliant. Can you expand?\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: Search engines downrank some abusive searches but inconsistently—they do just enough to look responsible while the material remains findable.\n\n\n## Enforcement, reporting and operational gaps\n> Witnesses said Ofcom must act faster, use stronger powers, improve reporting routes, and support trusted flaggers and survivors.\n\nThe Lord Bishop of Winchester: Do the Act and codes tackle platform design and business models that enable VAWG, and could Ofcom force design changes now?\nJanaya Walker: No: the system looks at individual pieces of content rather than the broader context and platform design. We need a safety-by-design code.\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: Search engines could and should make clearer choices to reduce abuse; the inconsistent blocking shows it’s a choice not a technical impossibility.\nLord Knight of Weymouth: How is Ofcom doing on enforcement and reporting? Sophie, you run the helpline—what are the practical problems?\nSophie Mortimer: Enforcement is too slow. Intimate images spread in hours; formal investigation and business disruption can take months. Reporting routes are clunky.\nSophie Mortimer: Some platforms tell us they don’t consider themselves ‘high risk’ and therefore out of scope—so they effectively mark their own homework.\nJanaya Walker: We supported a survivor who pressed Ofcom only after long delays; legal pressure prompted faster action. Survivors shouldn’t have to litigate to get enforcement.\nLord Knight of Weymouth: Would an e-safety commissioner or civil redress help?\nJanaya Walker: Yes. An online abuse commission could represent survivors and help co‑ordinate responses; specialist services are underfunded and need ring-fenced support.\nAlexandra Whiston-Dew: We need to ask why stronger, quicker steps—like content switch-off with an appeals process—aren’t used. Some content causes real-world physical risk.\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: Two enforcement gaps: Ofcom hasn’t acted on extreme porn content and it took months to act on AI tool Grok; its conservative interpretation of the Act limits action.\nBaroness Owen of Alderley Edge: The Crime and Policing Act added powers for a central hash registry and a 48‑hour takedown. Sophie, where is that scoping work?\nSophie Mortimer: The Home Office scoping just finished and will report recommendations to Ministers. We’re pressing for quick next steps.\nBaroness Owen of Alderley Edge: Will hashing plus 48-hour takedown be enough?\nSophie Mortimer: They help. Hashing blocks reuploads and is privacy-preserving. But gaps remain: private messaging, services outside scope, existing copies already online, and sites that ignore reports.\nSophie Mortimer: We want services to check existing content against new hashes, treat reported originals and altered copies separately with clear deadlines, and fast business disruption for non-compliant sites.\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: Also, individuals have no straightforward civil right to demand takedown here. Other countries have swift civil remedies that give victims immediate orders and some compensation.\nJanaya Walker: Trusted flagger routes work when platforms respond, but capacity for those specialist organisations is limited and removals are dropping in some cases.\n\n\n## New harms and clear priorities\n> AI, chatbots, apps and wearable tech are creating new harms; experts propose safety-by-design, stronger codes, faster enforcement and better victim redress.\n\nLord Kirkhope of Harrogate: Has the regime kept pace with new harms like AI? Which should be priorities?\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: Not yet. AI chatbots create a new problem: some services let users create simulated characters for incest or rape role-play—this isn’t yet covered effectively.\nSophie Mortimer: The law so far reacts to each new tool. Synthetic sexual content is up sharply; callers say they first seek help from generative chatbots, which may give poor advice.\nJanaya Walker: Wearable tech and covert recording are other emerging harms. We need safety-by-design rules so platforms consider these risks before products are released.\nAlexandra Whiston-Dew: Law reform is useful but slow. Police guidance, public dialogue, and quicker operational tools matter too—training and recognition of how old laws apply to new tech.\nBaroness Fleet: If you each had to pick two priority recommendations, what would they be?\nProfessor Clare McGlynn: 1) Better regulation of mainstream porn sites with an auditor or regulator to police extreme content; 2) Bring app stores into scope so harmful tools can’t spread easily.\nSophie Mortimer: 1) A true one-and-done: verified hashes should block content across a service and include existing copies; 2) Faster, clearer powers against non-compliant sites and statutory access for hosts and domains.\nJanaya Walker: 1) Upgrade the VAWG guidance into a binding code and create a safety-by-design code; 2) Set up an online commission for survivors and ring-fence funds from digital taxes for specialist services.\nAlexandra Whiston-Dew: 1) More transparency about Ofcom’s decision process so complainants know progress; 2) Empower quicker, decisive action like switch-off with appeal to prevent serious harm.\nChair: Thank you all. You’ve been concise and detailed. Your practical suggestions on binding codes, faster enforcement, safety-by-design, better reporting and survivor redress come through strongly.\n",
